nodes.body:
Hello everyone!
In multi-tenant applications (especially those using PostgreSQL schema-per-tenant), we need to determine which tenant context to use for database operations. With the current API, the authenticate and get_user methods have no access to the request context, making it difficult to properly route database queries to the correct tenant schema.

I was wondering if it would be possible (*maybe im wrong and there's already a better solution in place*) to add context-aware variants of the authenticate and get_user methods to the `AuthnBackend` trait.

```rust
#[async_trait]
pub trait AuthnBackend: Clone + Send + Sync {
    // Existing methods retained for backward compatibility
    async fn authenticate(&self, creds: Self::Credentials) -> Result<Option<Self::User>, Self::Error>;
    async fn get_user(&self, user_id: &UserId<Self>) -> Result<Option<Self::User>, Self::Error>;
    
    // New context-aware methods with default implementations for backward compatibility
    #[allow(unused_variables)]
    async fn authenticate_with_context(
        &self,
        creds: Self::Credentials,
        extensions: &Extensions,
    ) -> Result<Option<Self::User>, Self::Error> {
        // Default implementation falls back to the original method
        self.authenticate(creds).await
    }
    
    #[allow(unused_variables)]
    async fn get_user_with_context(
        &self,
        user_id: &UserId<Self>,
        extensions: &Extensions,
    ) -> Result<Option<Self::User>, Self::Error> {
        // Default implementation falls back to the original method
        self.get_user(user_id).await
    }
}
```

And to modify `AuthSession` to provide methods that utilize these context-aware variants:

```rust
impl<Backend: AuthnBackend> AuthSession<Backend> {
    // New method to create from session with context
    pub(crate) async fn from_session_with_context(
        session: Session,
        backend: Backend,
        data_key: &'static str,
        extensions: &Extensions,
    ) -> Result<Self, Error<Backend>> {
        // Implementation using get_user_with_context
    }
```

It would be really helpful for our use case, but I can also see it being helpful in other scenarios where you might need to use additional context passed previously through the pipeline.
